<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="stock-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="stock-title">Why the stock figure matters so much</h2>
      <p>Profit depends on closing stock. If the year-end stock is overstated, profit rises on paper. If understated, profit falls. Neither reflects what really happened, and a bank or tax officer may ask questions.</p>
      <p class="para">Ask your accountant how raw material, work in progress and finished goods are being valued, and whether the method follows the applicable accounting standard. Keep the method consistent, and keep the count sheets.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Job work and GST</h2>
      <p>If you send goods to a job worker, you are the principal, and section 143 of the CGST Act puts specific duties on you:</p>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Keeping proper accounts for what you send out. That responsibility stays with you</li>
        <li>Getting inputs back, or supplying them from the job worker's place, within one year</li>
        <li>Doing the same for capital goods within three years. Moulds, dies, jigs, fixtures and tools are outside this limit</li>
        <li>Goods not back in time are treated as supplied by you on the day they were sent out. The Commissioner can extend the period for sufficient cause</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="para">Secondary sources also describe delivery challans and Form ITC-04. We did not read the rules behind these, so check how often to file on the portal, and ask your adviser about credit and interest if a deadline is missed.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="monthly-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="monthly-title" class="first-h2">What monthly reporting should show</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Material used compared with production, for your main products</li>
        <li>Gross margin after material and direct labour</li>
        <li>Stock at each stage, with slow-moving items</li>
        <li>Material at job workers, and how long it has been out</li>
        <li>Wages and overtime compared with output</li>
      </ul>
      <p>You do not need a costing system on day one. Begin with your top three products and one simple sum: what went in, what came out, and what it sold for.</p>

      <h2 id="staff">Staff and compliance</h2>
      <p>Factory staff add {% routeLink "/services/payroll-services/", "payroll" %} with attendance and overtime records, and PF and ESI where they apply. Payments to contractors may bring {% routeLink "/services/tds-return-filing/", "TDS" %}. Machinery needs a fixed asset register so that depreciation can be worked out. Larger companies may have cost record or audit requirements, so ask whether they apply to yours.</p>

      <h2 id="ask">What to ask an accountant</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Have you worked with businesses that convert materials?</li>
        <li>Will you record material issues and production, or just purchases and sales?</li>
        <li>How will you value stock and work in progress each month?</li>
        <li>How do you track material at job workers?</li>
        <li>Can you set up stock items and units in my software?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="where">Where units are based</h2>
      <p>For more on Delhi's estates, see {% routeLink "/locations/industrial-manufacturing/", "the industrial and manufacturing hub" %}. The accounting workflow described here does not depend on your location.</p>

